.TH CCOL_SELECT 3 "2026-10-01" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
ccol_select, ccol_select_timed \- wait for readiness on any of several queues or file descriptors (blocking)
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cthreadcomm.h>

ccol_retval_t ccol_select(size_t *ready_index, size_t n,
                        ccol_selectable *selectables);

ccol_retval_t ccol_select_timed(size_t *ready_index, size_t n,
                        ccol_selectable *selectables, uint64_t timeout_us);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
The call blocks the thread of the caller until at least one of the n
selectables becomes ready.  It then writes the 0-based index of that
selectable into *ready_index and returns.

ccol_select() never does the receive or the send itself, for any type of
selectable.  It only reports readiness.  The caller must then make its own
non-blocking receive or send call on the selectable that won.  It must do
that at once.  For a queue selectable the call is
ccol_circq_try_recv_zc(), ccol_dynmq_try_recv_zc(),
ccol_circq_try_send_zc() or ccol_dynmq_send_zc().  For an fd selectable it
is read(2), recv(2), write(2) or send(2) on
selectables[*ready_index].fd.  A concurrent consumer or producer can win
the race between the return of ccol_select() and that call.  This is the
same time-of-check to time-of-use contract that POSIX select(2) has.  The
call must therefore be non-blocking, and the caller must check its result.

ccol_select_timed() takes one more argument, timeout_us.  A value of
UINT64_MAX behaves in the same way as ccol_select(), which waits forever.
A value of 0 makes a poll that does not block.  Any other value is the
maximum number of microseconds to wait before the call returns
ccol_timed_out.  A value too large to form a deadline saturates to the
latest deadline that the clock can hold, and never wraps into the past.
The library computes the deadline once, before the loop, from
CLOCK_MONOTONIC.  A change of the system time can therefore not extend
the timeout.

ccol_select() is the same as ccol_select_timed(ready_index, n,
selectables, UINT64_MAX).

A ccol_selectable holds a ccol_circular_queue, a ccol_dynamic_queue, or a
raw file descriptor.  Build one with ccol_selectable_from_circq(),
ccol_selectable_from_dynq(), ccol_selectable_from_chan(), or
ccol_selectable_from_fd().  A channel resolves to the circular queue below
it before it comes here.  ccol_select() therefore treats a channel exactly
as a plain circular queue.

When there is at least one fd selectable, the call watches the file
descriptors.  It uses an epoll(7) instance on Linux, or a kqueue(2) on
the BSDs.  The call creates that instance with close-on-exec, and closes
it before it returns.  The call watches each fd for the full call, in
level-triggered mode, in the direction of its selectable.  The call never
changes or closes the fd itself.  A call that has only queue selectables
creates no file descriptor.

The call allocates a small quantity of memory for each selectable.  It
frees all of that memory on every return path.  Any number of threads can
call ccol_select() on the same queue at the same time.

A producer wakes exactly one waiter for each message that it sends or each
slot that it frees.  It never wakes all of them at once.  A caller can stop
watching a queue while that queue is still ready for its direction.  That
caller then forwards the wake to the next waiter.  The notification that it
got is therefore never absorbed.  This holds for every way of leaving the
call: another selectable becomes ready, the deadline elapses, an fd fires,
or the call returns an error.  No waiter stays parked on a queue that has
something for it.

The call checks each selectable at least one time before it waits.  The
call therefore reports a selectable that is ready at the start, also with
a timeout_us of 0.  The call loses no wakeup.  A send or a free between
that check and the wait also stops the wait.  A spurious wakeup does not
stop the wait early, and it does not make the deadline later.  A signal
does not do these things either.  On the fd path, the call rounds the
remaining time up to whole milliseconds.  The wait therefore never stops
before the deadline.  After the deadline, the call checks the file
descriptors one last time.  Then it gives ccol_timed_out.  When the wait
itself fails, the call gives ccol_unexpected_failure.

The call takes its locks in one fixed order.  It therefore cannot
deadlock with a producer, with a consumer, or with a different
ccol_select() on the same queues.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
ready_index   Pointer to a size_t.  It gets the 0-based index of the
              selectable that became ready.  It is valid only when the
              call returns ccol_success.
n             Number of elements in the selectables array.  It must be
              >= 1 and no larger than INT_MAX.  The call rejects any
              other value with ccol_invalid_args (see RETURN VALUE).  It
              must also be no larger than SIZE_MAX / sizeof(internal
              waiter node).  No realistic caller can reach either
              ceiling.
selectables   Array of n ccol_selectable values to watch.
timeout_us    For ccol_select_timed only.  Maximum microseconds to wait.
              UINT64_MAX waits forever, the same as ccol_select.  0
              makes a poll that does not block, and still reports every
              selectable that is ready at the moment of the call.  Any
              other value waits at least that many microseconds before
              it reports a timeout.
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
ccol_success            A selectable is ready and *ready_index is set.
                        The caller must then make its own receive or
                        send call (see DESCRIPTION).

ccol_timed_out          For ccol_select_timed only.  timeout_us elapsed
                        before any selectable became ready.
                        *ready_index does not change.

ccol_invalid_args       One of these holds.  ready_index or selectables
                        is NULL.  n is 0.  n is larger than INT_MAX,
                        because the library narrows the index of a
                        matched selectable through a plain int.  n is
                        larger than SIZE_MAX / sizeof(internal waiter
                        node), because the internal waiter-node array,
                        which has one node for each selectable, would
                        overflow size_t.  No n that a caller can
                        realistically build reaches either of those two
                        ceilings.  A queue selectable holds a NULL queue
                        pointer.  An fd selectable holds a negative fd.
                        Or a type tag is present that the library does
                        not know.
ccol_not_enough_memory  malloc failed to allocate the internal waiter
                        node array.  That array has one node for each
                        selectable.
ccol_unexpected_failure epoll_create1(2), eventfd(2), or epoll_ctl(2)
                        failed with an errno that the library does not
                        expect.  Both functions can return this, but
                        only when at least one fd selectable is present.
                        ccol_select_timed() also returns it in one more
                        case.  That case needs no fd selectable and a
                        timeout_us other than UINT64_MAX.  The internal
                        timed wait on a condition variable then fails
                        with an error that is not a timeout.  Treat this
                        as a fatal error in the configuration.  For
                        example, the fd table is full, or the memory is
                        under pressure.  An fd selectable that names a
                        file that epoll(7) cannot watch also gives this
                        code, on every call.  A regular file and a
                        directory are such files: epoll_ctl(2) refuses
                        them with EPERM, where poll(2) and select(2)
                        report them as always ready.  The FreeBSD build
                        also refuses them, although kqueue(2) can watch
                        them.  Therefore, one program operates in the same
                        way on the two systems.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
The library allocates the waiter nodes on the heap, one for each
selectable, for as long as the call lasts.  It frees them on every return
path, and an error path is one of those.  A large n therefore never
causes an overflow of the stack.

Any number of threads can call ccol_select() on the same queue at the same
time.  The waiter list is a linked list that grows as needed.  It has no
fixed capacity.

Several ccol_select() threads can watch a queue that becomes ready.  The
producer then wakes only the head waiter, which is a single wake.  The
waiter that wakes forwards the notify to the next waiter in line before it
returns.  It always does that.  It does not depend on whether its own
receive or send call succeeds.  Every waiter that is interested therefore
gets a chance to observe the same readiness.  That is a cascade of wakes.
This stops a thundering herd.  It also stops a gap in liveness.  Such a gap
happens when several messages arrive, or several slots become free, one
after the other in a short time.

A caller can turn writing off on a queue.  ccol_circq_disable_sending() or
ccol_dynmq_disable_sending() does that.  Read-direction waiters on that
queue stay registered and keep waiting.  ccol_select() unblocks once
sending is on again and a message arrives.  The library wakes
write-direction waiters at once, so they can observe the new state.

Every file descriptor must stay open for the whole ccol_select() call.  A
close of an fd that ccol_select() watches now is undefined behaviour.

A queue or a ccol_channel that goes to ccol_selectable_from_circq(),
ccol_selectable_from_dynq() or ccol_selectable_from_chan() has the same
rule.  Nothing may destroy it while a ccol_select() or ccol_select_timed()
call still watches it.  ccol_circular_queue_destroy(),
ccol_dynamic_queue_destroy() and ccol_channel_destroy() assert when the
queue still has a linked waiter.  That waiter comes from a
ccol_select() or ccol_select_timed() call that is in progress, or from a
live ccol_event_loop registration.  Without that assert, the waiter node
that is still linked points to the mutex of the queue, which the destroy is
about to free.

The same fd can appear in more than one selectable inside one call.  The
directions can be mixed in any way, and an exact duplicate of the same
direction twice is allowed too.  One example is a watch on one connected
socket for readability and writability at once.  The library combines
selectables that share an fd into one registration below.  When that
registration fires, ccol_select() resolves the index that it gives back.
It walks the selectables that share the fd in the order that the caller
gave them.  It picks the first one whose own direction is really satisfied
by what became ready.

The library uses POSIX mutexes and condition variables inside.  It uses
epoll(7) and eventfd(2) on Linux when there is an fd selectable.  It uses
kqueue(2) and eventfd(2) on FreeBSD in the same condition.  This page gives
the names of the epoll(7) calls of the Linux build.  The FreeBSD build
operates in the same way.
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
/* Fan-in from two circular queues and a ccol_channel: */
size_t idx;
ccol_retval_t r = ccol_select(&idx, 3,
    (ccol_selectable[]){
        ccol_selectable_from_circq(q0, ccol_select_read),
        ccol_selectable_from_circq(q1, ccol_select_read),
        ccol_selectable_from_chan(ch, ccol_select_read),
    });
if (r == ccol_success) {
    printf("selectable %zu is ready\en", idx);
}

/* Equivalent using the convenience macro: */
r = ccol_select_va(&idx,
        ccol_selectable_from_circq(q0, ccol_select_read),
        ccol_selectable_from_circq(q1, ccol_select_read),
        ccol_selectable_from_chan(ch, ccol_select_read));

/* Mixed read+write: wake when q0 has room OR dq has a message: */
r = ccol_select_va(&idx,
        ccol_selectable_from_circq(q0, ccol_select_write),
        ccol_selectable_from_dynq(dq,  ccol_select_read));

/* Fan-in from a queue and a pipe read end: */
int pfd[2];
if (pipe(pfd) != 0) { perror("pipe"); exit(1); }
r = ccol_select_va(&idx,
        ccol_selectable_from_circq(q0, ccol_select_read),
        ccol_selectable_from_fd(pfd[0], ccol_select_read));
if (r == ccol_success) {
    c_message_t msg;
    if (idx == 0) {
        /* queue is ready; claim it ourselves (zero-copy) */
        if (ccol_circq_try_recv_zc(q0, &msg) == ccol_success) {
            printf("queue message size: %zu\en", msg.size);
            free(msg.data);
        }
    } else {
        /* fd is ready; read it ourselves */
        char buf[512];
        ssize_t n = read(pfd[0], buf, sizeof(buf));
        printf("read %zd bytes from fd\en", n);
    }
}

/* Timed wait with 500 ms deadline: */
r = ccol_select_timed_va(&idx, 500000,
        ccol_selectable_from_circq(q0, ccol_select_read));
if (r == ccol_timed_out) {
    printf("no source ready within 500 ms\en");
}

/* Watch one connected socket for either direction, whichever comes first: */
r = ccol_select_va(&idx,
        ccol_selectable_from_fd(sock_fd, ccol_select_read),
        ccol_selectable_from_fd(sock_fd, ccol_select_write));
if (r == ccol_success) {
    if (idx == 0) {
        char buf[512];
        ssize_t n = read(sock_fd, buf, sizeof(buf));
        printf("read %zd bytes\en", n);
    } else if (write(sock_fd, "hi", 2) < 0) {
        perror("write");  /* EAGAIN: a different writer used the room */
    }
}
.fi
